concepts.body:
An **async function** always returns a promise. Inside it, **`await`** pauses the function until a promise settles, then gives you the fulfilled value — or throws the rejection reason:

```javascript
async function showOrders(id) {
  try {
    const user = await loadUser(id);
    const orders = await loadOrders(user.id);
    render(orders);
  } catch (err) {
    showError(err);
  }
}
```

It's still promises underneath: the `return` value becomes the promise's value and a `throw` rejects it. Pausing doesn't freeze anything: other code runs while the function waits. `await` also works at the top level of modules.

The most important speed habit: **don't make independent steps wait for each other**. Each `await` waits before the next line starts, so

```javascript
const a = await getA();
const b = await getB();
```

takes the sum of both durations. If they don't depend on each other, start both and await together: `const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getA(), getB()])`.

Loops need care:

- `for...of` with `await` inside handles items **one after another** — correct when order matters, or when a server limits how fast you may send requests.
- `array.forEach(async ...)` does **not** wait; `forEach` ignores the returned promises, so the code after it runs immediately and errors go unhandled.
- To handle all items at the same time, `await Promise.all(items.map(async item => ...))`.

Calling an async function without `await` (or `.catch`) "fires and forgets" it; any error becomes an unhandled rejection. Code-checking tools (linters) can warn about these forgotten promises. Inside `try`, write `return await p` if you want that `catch` to handle `p`'s rejection.

concepts.code:
const sleep = (ms, v) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));

async function getUser(id) { await sleep(50); return { id, name: "Ada" }; }
async function getPosts(id) { await sleep(50); return ["hello", "world"]; }
async function getFails() { await sleep(10); throw new Error("not found"); }

async function main() {
  let t = Date.now();
  const user = await getUser(1);
  const posts = await getPosts(1);                 // waits for getUser first
  console.log("sequential ~100ms:", Date.now() - t >= 100);

  t = Date.now();
  const [u2, p2] = await Promise.all([getUser(1), getPosts(1)]);
  console.log("parallel ~50ms:", Date.now() - t < 100); // true

  try {
    await getFails();
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("caught:", err.message);           // caught: not found
  }
  return `${user.name} has ${posts.length} posts`;
}

main().then(console.log);                          // Ada has 2 posts

concepts.body:
Apps get data by sending a **request** to a **server** — a computer that waits for requests and answers them — and reading its **response**. These messages follow the web's rules, called **HTTP**. `fetch(url, options)` sends a request to a **URL** (a web address) and returns a promise for the response. It's built into browsers and into Node.js 18+.

```javascript
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/users/1");
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const user = await res.json();
```

The crucial detail: fetch only **rejects when no response arrives at all** (offline, unknown address, blocked by the browser, cancelled). Every response carries a **status code**, a three-digit number such as 200 (OK), 404 (not found) or 500 (server error). A response reporting a problem still *fulfils*, so you must check `res.ok` (true for 200–299) or `res.status` yourself.

Reading the response's content (its body) is a second step that also needs `await`: `res.json()`, `res.text()`, or `res.blob()` for files such as images. A body can only be read once.

To send data, set the **HTTP method** (`"POST"` means "here is new data"; the default, `"GET"`, just asks), the headers (labels describing the request, such as the kind of data you're sending) and the body (the data itself):

```javascript
await fetch("/api/todos", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: "Write docs" }),
});
```

For form uploads, pass a `FormData` as the body and let fetch label the data for you. To add `?name=value` options to a URL (its **query string**), build it with `new URL(...)` and `url.searchParams.set(...)`, which safely handles spaces and special characters.

**Cancelling** a request uses an `AbortSignal`: create `const controller = new AbortController()`, pass `{ signal: controller.signal }` and call `controller.abort()`; or pass `AbortSignal.timeout(5000)` to give up after 5 seconds. The promise then rejects with an `AbortError` or `TimeoutError`.

In browsers, a page may only read responses from another website if that website allows it; these rules are called **CORS**. If it doesn't, the browser blocks the response and fetch rejects with a generic `TypeError`. **Cookies** — small pieces of data a site keeps in your browser, such as your login — are sent with requests to your own site by default; other sites need `credentials: "include"`.

